│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_path_redundancy_elimination_broadcast                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm sends each partition of the data from src to every destination      │
│                          │ independently via Dijkstra, so shared link segments are traversed once per (dst,          │
│                          │ partition) — causing massive redundant transfers and high cost. Key improvement: treat    │
│                          │ the broadcast as a Steiner-tree-like shared delivery problem. For each partition, find a  │
│                          │ set of paths to all destinations that maximally share edges, so each shared edge is only  │
│                          │ paid for once across the destinations it serves. Concretely: (1) add a small epsilon to   │
│                          │ all edge costs so Dijkstra prefers fewer hops (fewer, longer-shared links), (2) greedily  │
│                          │ route destinations one at a time over the cheapest available path in a residual graph     │
│                          │ where edges already used for this partition are free (cost ~0) — this reuses shared trunk │
│                          │ links and only pays for the diverging tails, (3) drop unreachable destinations gracefully │
│                          │ instead of crashing. This dramatically reduces total data transfer cost while keeping the │
│                          │ API (BroadCastTopology, append_dst_partition_path) unchanged.                             │
