│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ greedy_steiner_tree_reuse_routing                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm routes each partition of each destination independently with only   │
│                          │ intra-destination congestion awareness, paying full cost on every edge for every          │
│                          │ partition. Since this is a broadcast (multicast) problem, edges already used by a prior   │
│                          │ destination's route can carry additional traffic at near-zero marginal cost. The          │
│                          │ best-performing prior generations (1035→1025 costs) showed that candidate selection and   │
│                          │ per-partition spreading help, but none combined (a) cross-destination edge reuse via a    │
│                          │ Steiner-tree-style heuristic with (b) per-partition load spreading. I implement a         │
│                          │ shortest-path-union Steiner heuristic: maintain a working "tree" graph whose edges, once  │
│                          │ paid for by any destination, cost a small epsilon for all subsequent destinations and     │
│                          │ partitions. Each partition of each destination runs Dijkstra on this reduced-cost working │
│                          │ copy; since tree edges are nearly free, later destinations naturally route through the    │
│                          │ existing broadcast tree instead of paying for fresh parallel links, eliminating redundant │
│                          │ transfers. After routing, each used edge's cost in the working copy drops to epsilon, so  │
│                          │ the union of paths approximates a directed Steiner tree. Edges are still emitted exactly  │
│                          │ as `[s, t, G]` via `append_dst_partition_path`, and the original G/h are never mutated,   │
│                          │ avoiding the fatal failure modes of earlier generations. The per-partition load counter   │
│                          │ is retained (with a mild congestion penalty on the working copy) so the num_partitions    │
│                          │ copies of each partition still spread across parallel paths when beneficial.              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0160                                                                                   │
